-- title:
--   Joint two-level semistable specialisation with degeneracy and Hecke transport
-- statement:
--   **Data and hypotheses.** Let $M,s,q'$ be non-zero natural numbers with $s$ and $q'$ prime, $s\neq q'$, $q'\nmid M$ and $s\nmid M$, and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}=$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` satisfying `A.LiesOverPrime q'`, that is, $q'$ is a non-unit of $A$. Write $k=$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField A`. For a level $N$, `modularFunctionFieldC k N` is the subfield of the Laurent series field over $k$ generated by `jqModC k` and `jqNModC k N`, and `ssPlaces q' N k` is the set of places $w$ of this field over $k$ satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace q' N k`; the two sets `ssPlaces q' (M*s) k` and `ssPlaces q' M k` are assumed finite (with decidable equality, as is $k$). Further, `JZero N` denotes the degree-zero divisor class group [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L223) of `modularFunctionFieldBar N` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones); at the levels $(Ms)q'$, $Ms$, $Mq'$ and $M$ it carries the module structure over `HeckeAlg = MvPolynomial Nat.Primes ℤ` given by [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleBar`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L82), and `modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)`, `modularFunctionFieldC k M` carry their $k$-algebra structures. Finally, `inertiaInvariants A N` is the subgroup of `JZero N` fixed by `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, the image in $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$; `PrimeToTorsion q' x` says that $n\cdot x=0$ for some $n>0$ with $q'\nmid n$; and `toricMonodromyPart q' I` is the `HeckeAlg`-submodule spanned by the elements $\sigma\cdot x-x$ with $\sigma\in I$ and $x$ killed by some positive integer coprime to $q'$.
--
--   **Asserted data.** There exist: `HeckeAlg`-module structures on [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s))`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L223) and on [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k M)`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L223) (asserted to exist, with no further property attached to them); semistable specialisation data $D_1$ of type `JZeroSemistableSpecialization A (M*s) q' hq'` and $D_2$ of type `JZeroSemistableSpecialization A M q' hq'` (each consisting of a finite set `nodes` of pairs of places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` with rational residue fields, a semilinear automorphism `frob` whose base automorphism is $a\mapsto a^{q'}$ and which stabilises the nodes with involutive induced permutation, a width function `width : nodes → ℕ`, a homomorphism `comp` from `inertiaInvariants A (N*q')` to the component group `componentGroup width` attached to the widths, a homomorphism `sp` from `inertiaInvariants A (N*q')` to [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k N) nodes`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L201), and the compatibility fields of the structure); a pair of maps $ab\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to($`ssPlaces q' (M*s) k`$\to$`ssPlaces q' M k`$)$; bijections $e_1\colon$`ssPlaces q' (M*s) k`$\simeq D_1.\mathrm{nodes}$ and $e_2\colon$`ssPlaces q' M k`$\simeq D_2.\mathrm{nodes}$; positive-integer-valued weight functions $w$ on `ssPlaces q' (M*s) k` and $w_V$ on `ssPlaces q' M k`; multiplicities $m\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to($`ssPlaces q' (M*s) k`$\to\mathbb N)$; a pair $dpair$ of additive maps `JZero ((M*s)*q') →+ JZero (M*q')`; and a pair $\Phi$ of additive maps `GluedPic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)) D₁.nodes →+ GluedPic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k M) D₂.nodes`, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   **Node sets and indexing.** $D_1.\mathrm{nodes}$ equals `nodePairsOfPlaces D₁.frob` applied to the finset of supersingular places at level $Ms$, namely the image of that finset under the embedding `smulNodePairEmb D₁.frob` attached to $D_1.\mathrm{frob}$, and likewise $D_2.\mathrm{nodes} =$`nodePairsOfPlaces D₂.frob` of the finset of supersingular places at level $M$. For every supersingular place $p$ at level $Ms$ the first coordinate of the node $e_1(p)$ is $p$, and for every supersingular place $p$ at level $M$ the first coordinate of $e_2(p)$ is $p$.
--
--   **Inertia differences are toric.** For every $\sigma\in$ `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` and every $x\in$ `JZero ((M*s)*q')` with `PrimeToTorsion q' x`, the element $\sigma\cdot x-x$ lies in `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')` and, as such, has $D_1.\mathrm{comp}$-image $0$ and its $D_1.\mathrm{sp}$-image has trivial image under [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.toPic0Pair D₁.nodes`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L229). The corresponding statement holds for $D_2$ with `JZero (M*q')` and `inertiaInvariants A (M*q')`.
--
--   **Degeneracy maps.** For each $i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, $dpair\ i$ carries `toricMonodromyPart q' (A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)` inside `JZero ((M*s)*q')` into `toricMonodromyPart q' (A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)` inside `JZero (M*q')`. For each $i$, each $x\in$ `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')` with $dpair\ i\ x\in$ `inertiaInvariants A (M*q')`, and $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$, one has $D_2.\mathrm{sp}(dpair\ i\ x)=\Phi\ i\ (D_1.\mathrm{sp}\,x)$; and under the same hypotheses $D_2.\mathrm{comp}(dpair\ i\ x)=0$. Moreover, for each $i$, each $y\in$ `JZero ((M*s)*q')` and $z\in$ `JZero ((M*q')*s)` whose identification along the equality $(Ms)q'=(Mq')s$ given by `Nat.mul_right_comm M s q'` matches $y$ with $z$, one has $dpair\ i\ y =$`degeneracyPushforwardPair (M*q') s i z`, the $i$-th of the two pushforward maps `JZero ((M*q')*s) →+ JZero (M*q')` along `heckeAlphaBar`, resp. `heckeBetaBar`, for level $Mq'$ and prime $s$ (this pair being $0$ when the inputs `DegeneracyPushforwardInputs` fail).
--
--   **Masses, weights and node units.** For each $i$ and each supersingular place $p$ at level $Ms$, $m\ i\ p\cdot w(p) = w_V(ab\ i\ p)$; for each $i$ and each supersingular place $v$ at level $M$, $\sum_{p:\ ab\ i\ p=v} m\ i\ p = s+1$. For each $i$ and each $g\colon D_1.\mathrm{nodes}\to$ `Additive kˣ`,
--   $$\Phi\ i\ (\mathrm{nodeUnit}\ D_1.\mathrm{nodes}\ g) = \mathrm{nodeUnit}\ D_2.\mathrm{nodes}\Bigl(n_2\mapsto \sum_{p_1:\ ab\ i\ p_1 = e_2^{-1}(n_2)} m\ i\ p_1\cdot g(e_1 p_1)\Bigr),$$
--   where [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.nodeUnit`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L247) is the map from node-unit data to the glued Picard group. The weights are the widths: $w(p)=D_1.\mathrm{width}(e_1 p)$ for every supersingular place $p$ at level $Ms$, and $w_V(v)=D_2.\mathrm{width}(e_2 v)$ for every supersingular place $v$ at level $M$.
--
--   **The nine-clause package at level $Ms$.** (1) The inertia-difference clause for $D_1$ stated above is repeated. (2) For every $m\in\mathbb N$ coprime to $q'$ and every $g$ in `GluedPic0 k (modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)) D₁.nodes` with $m\cdot g=0$ there is an $x\in$ `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')` with $m\cdot x=0$, $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$ and $D_1.\mathrm{sp}\,x=g$. (3) For every $m$ coprime to $q'$ and every $\varphi\in$ `componentGroup D₁.width` with $m\cdot\varphi=0$ there is an $x\in$ `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')` with $m\cdot x=0$ and $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=\varphi$. (4) $D_1.\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective. (5) For every $\sigma$ in `A.decompositionSubgroup ℚ` and every inertia-invariant $x$ with $\sigma\cdot x$ again inertia-invariant, $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$ implies $D_1.\mathrm{comp}(\sigma\cdot x)=0$. (6) Under the same hypotheses, if in addition `toPic0Pair D₁.nodes (D₁.sp x) = 0` then `toPic0Pair D₁.nodes (D₁.sp (σ • x)) = 0`. (7) Under the same hypotheses, for all $a,b\in$ `JZero (M*s)`, if `toPic0Pair D₁.nodes (D₁.sp x)` $=(D_1.\mathrm{spN}\,a, D_1.\mathrm{spN}\,b)$ then `toPic0Pair D₁.nodes (D₁.sp (σ • x))` $=(D_1.\mathrm{spN}(\sigma\cdot a), D_1.\mathrm{spN}(\sigma\cdot b))$, where $D_1.\mathrm{spN}$ is the corresponding component of the specialisation data. (8) Every node has positive width. (9) There is a `HeckeAlg`-module structure on `componentGroup D₁.width` for which $D_1.\mathrm{comp}$ is equivariant, i.e. $D_1.\mathrm{comp}(T\cdot x)=T\cdot D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x$ for every $T\in$ `HeckeAlg` and every inertia-invariant $x$ with $T\cdot x$ inertia-invariant, and which satisfies the following torsion clause: for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of `HeckeAlg` with `heckeTorsion (componentGroup D₁.width) 𝔪 = ⊥`, every $x$ in `heckeTorsion (JZero ((M*s)*q')) 𝔪` satisfying `PrimeToTorsion q' x`, lying in `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')`, with $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$ and `toPic0Pair D₁.nodes (D₁.sp x) = 0`, lies in `toricMonodromyPart q' (A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)`.
--
--   **The nine-clause package at level $M$.** The same nine clauses hold verbatim for $D_2$, with `JZero ((M*s)*q')` replaced by `JZero (M*q')`, `inertiaInvariants A ((M*s)*q')` by `inertiaInvariants A (M*q')`, `modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)` by `modularFunctionFieldC k M`, `JZero (M*s)` by `JZero M`, and $D_1$ by $D_2$ throughout.
--
--   **Hecke transport on node units.** For every prime $\ell$ there are an integer matrix $T_\ell$ indexed by $D_1.\mathrm{nodes}\times D_1.\mathrm{nodes}$ and an integer $n_\ell$ such that $\sum_{t} T_\ell\,t\,s = n_\ell$ for every node $s$, and such that, if $\ell\neq q'$, then $n_\ell$ equals [`AlgebraicCurve.finrankAlong k (heckeAlphaC k (M*s) ℓ)`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L51), the degree of the roof field `charLDegeneracyRoof k (M*s) ℓ` over `modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)` along the inclusion `heckeAlphaC`, and, assuming that principal divisors exist for `charLDegeneracyRoof k (M*s) ℓ` and that `heckeAlphaC k (M*s) ℓ` and `heckeBetaC k (M*s) ℓ` are integral, $T_\ell\,a\,b$ is the coefficient at the place $a.1.1$ (the first coordinate of the node $a$) of [`AlgebraicCurve.Divisor.correspondence`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L137) for the pair (`heckeAlphaC`, `heckeBetaC`) — pullback along `heckeAlphaC` followed by pushforward along `heckeBetaC` — applied to the divisor `Finsupp.single b.1.1 1`. Furthermore, for every inertia-invariant $x$ with `heckeGen ℓ • x` inertia-invariant and $D_1.\mathrm{comp}\,x=0$, and every $w\colon D_1.\mathrm{nodes}\to$ `Additive kˣ` with $D_1.\mathrm{sp}\,x =$`nodeUnit D₁.nodes w`, one has $D_1.\mathrm{sp}(\mathrm{heckeGen}\ \ell\cdot x) =$`nodeUnit D₁.nodes` applied to $t\mapsto \sum_{s} T_\ell\,s\,t\cdot w(s)$; here `heckeGen ℓ` is the variable $X_\ell$ of `HeckeAlg`. The identical statement holds for $D_2$, at level $M$ and with `JZero (M*q')`.
--
--   **Identification of the maps $ab$.** For every pair $\varphi\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to($`modularFunctionFieldC k M` $\to_{\mathrm{alg}[k]}$ `modularFunctionFieldC k (M*s)`$)$ of $k$-algebra maps whose underlying ring homomorphisms are integral, if $\varphi\,0$ is the identity on Laurent series (i.e. $\varphi\,0\,x = x$ as Laurent series for all $x$) and $\varphi\,1$ acts as `qExpand k s`, then for each $i$ and each supersingular place $p$ at level $Ms$ one has [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.restrictAlong (φ i) (hφ i) p`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L204) $= ab\ i\ p$; that is, $ab\ 0$ and $ab\ 1$ are the restrictions of supersingular places along the two degeneracy maps.
--
--   **Widths in characteristic $q'$.** For every supersingular place $p$ at level $Ms$, $w(p)=$ `placeWidthChar q' (M*s) p`, and for every supersingular place $v$ at level $M$, $w_V(v)=$ `placeWidthChar q' M v`; here `placeWidthChar q N w` is `jWidthChar q (w.evalAt (jGeomGen K N))` divided by `placeRamificationJ N w`, the characteristic-sensitive width of the $j$-value of the place divided by its ramification index over the $j$-line.
--
--   This is the joint Čerednik–Drinfeld-style description of the characteristic-$q'$ semistable specialisation of the degree-zero Picard groups at the two levels $Ms$ and $M$ simultaneously: the nodes of both reductions are indexed by supersingular places, the component-group and glued-Picard specialisation maps are related across the levels by the two degeneracy pushforwards, the Hecke action is transported to integer matrices on the node lattices, and the gluing weights are identified with the characteristic-$q'$ place widths. It is the input, via its supersingular-place data, to [`ModularCurve.SSLevelDatum.eq_empty_or_eq_univ_of_forall_fst_mem_iff_snd_mem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.SSLevelDatum.eq_empty_or_eq_univ_of_forall_fst_mem_iff_snd_mem), and so feeds the level-lowering step of the Fermat argument.
